=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa1, dswa2
diagnosis_text:
The affected subsystem is the iptables FORWARD policy on both Office A distribution switches, dswa1 and dswa2. Identical rules were injected on both devices as "last night's change":

```
-A FORWARD -s 10.1.0.0/24 -d 10.3.0.0/24 -i br0.10 -p icmp -j ACCEPT
-A FORWARD -s 10.1.0.0/24 -d 10.3.0.0/24 -i br0.10 -j DROP
```

The mechanism is a permit-ICMP / deny-all policy applied to every packet that arrives from the Office A client VLAN (br0.10, 10.1.0.0/24) and is destined for the Office B VLAN-10 subnet (10.3.0.0/24). Because ICMP is explicitly accepted first, inter-office ping appears to work, masking the problem. All TCP and UDP traffic is then silently discarded by the catch-all DROP; the kernel never sends a reset, so client-side connections hang until their timeout fires. This is confirmed empirically: dswa1's DROP counter incremented by 2 packets / 120 bytes (exactly two TCP SYN frames) when a netcat connection was attempted from pc1 (10.1.0.100) to pc3 (10.3.0.100), while pc3 carries no local firewall whatsoever.

Localization: the fault lives in the iptables FORWARD chain on dswa1 (router-ID 10.0.0.79) and dswa2 (10.0.0.80), the two distribution switches serving all Office A access switches (aswa1–aswa3). The rule is applied on inbound interface br0.10 (Office A VLAN 10 SVI). The server at 10.5.0.4 lives in 10.5.0.0/24 and is not covered by this rule, so direct IP-level reachability tests to that host pass; however any portion of the payroll workflow that relies on resources in the 10.3.0.0/24 Office B subnet breaks silently.

Root cause: a pair of iptables FORWARD rules added on dswa1 and dswa2 during "last night's change" silently drops all non-ICMP traffic sourced from Office A clients (10.1.0.0/24) toward the Office B VLAN-10 subnet (10.3.0.0/24).

Corrective fix: on both dswa1 and dswa2, remove the offending rules:
`iptables -D FORWARD -s 10.1.0.0/24 -d 10.3.0.0/24 -i br0.10 -p icmp -j ACCEPT`
`iptables -D FORWARD -s 10.1.0.0/24 -d 10.3.0.0/24 -i br0.10 -j DROP`
If selective ICMP-only access to 10.3.0.0/24 was intentional, re-evaluate the policy and document it; otherwise remove both rules to restore full inter-office reachability.
=== END ===